/** Edited educator explanations: beginner to advanced, one original case per course-week. */
window.DALP_EDUCATOR_DEPTH = {"introduction":[{"week":1,"level":"Start here","foundation":"A business question is a decision you can investigate, not a vague topic. Identify who needs the answer, the metric, the reporting window, and what action they may take.","worked":"“Sales declined” is unclear; “Which region’s completed-order revenue fell most from May to June?” identifies a metric, comparison and segment.","extension":"Before opening a tool, write two rival explanations and list the fields needed to test each. Do not present an explanation as fact until the records support it.","evidence":"Submit one decision question, a source-field checklist and a sentence explaining what the answer cannot prove."},{"week":2,"level":"Start here","foundation":"A field is a column; a record is a row. Separate identifiers, categories, dates, counts and monetary measures before calculating anything. Numeric-looking customer IDs remain text.","worked":"In a CSV, customer_id CUST004 is an identifier, quantity 3 is a count, order_date is a date and unit_price_ngn is a currency amount.","extension":"Test an import that preserves leading zeroes, then document the expected type, unit and allowed values for every required field.","evidence":"Produce a five-field dictionary; verify that each column’s type matches its intended business meaning."},{"week":3,"level":"Build your skills","foundation":"Grain means what one row represents. A join or aggregation is only correct when you know whether rows represent orders, order lines, customers or months.","worked":"Two rows with the same order_id may be two product lines; COUNT rows is 2, while distinct orders is 1.","extension":"Sketch a one-to-many relationship and predict when joining a customer table to order lines would duplicate a customer-level balance.","evidence":"Define the grain of each input and the grain of the requested output before aggregating."},{"week":4,"level":"Build your skills","foundation":"Quality is measurable. Compare completeness, validity, consistency, uniqueness and timeliness rather than simply deleting records that look unusual.","worked":"If 7 of 200 orders have no region, missing-region rate is 7 / 200 = 3.5%; report the denominator and whether blanks affect the KPI.","extension":"Write a rule to flag negative quantities and unexpected status labels. Keep the original values and separately document any corrections.","evidence":"Deliver a small quality scorecard with issue counts, affected KPIs and a reproducible rule for every flag."},{"week":5,"level":"Build your skills","foundation":"An analytical KPI is a named calculation with an explicit population. Total sales, average order value and gross margin answer different business questions.","worked":"For two completed orders worth ₦12,000 and ₦8,000, revenue is ₦20,000 and average order value is ₦10,000, not the average unit price.","extension":"Calculate a rate per order and a margin percentage, then change a denominator deliberately to see why the metric changes.","evidence":"Provide formulas, units, status exclusions and one independent hand calculation."},{"week":6,"level":"Apply professionally","foundation":"A repeatable workflow separates raw, cleaned, modelled and reporting data. Transformations should be described so another analyst can recreate the result.","worked":"Instead of overwriting an invalid date, create a cleaned_date field, record the parsing rule and list unparseable rows.","extension":"Convert a one-off manual edit into a documented sequence of steps. Demonstrate that rerunning it on the same raw file yields the same output.","evidence":"Submit the raw-to-clean record count reconciliation and a log of changes."},{"week":7,"level":"Apply professionally","foundation":"A chart is an argument supported by data. Choose the graphic based on comparison, trend, distribution or relationship; its axis and labels affect interpretation.","worked":"Use a line chart for monthly sales and a sorted bar chart for region totals. A pie chart is less useful when categories have similar values.","extension":"Rebuild one misleading chart with a consistent scale, a meaningful title, data units and the actual reporting period.","evidence":"Explain the visual choice and verify at least one plotted value against its source table."},{"week":8,"level":"Apply professionally","foundation":"A dashboard should answer a small set of related decisions, not display every field. Lead with a KPI, then trend, category drivers and usable filters.","worked":"Place revenue, gross profit and target attainment above a monthly trend; let the region filter apply consistently to all related visuals.","extension":"Draw a filter-interaction map and test whether a selected month changes the intended visuals but not an unrelated explanatory note.","evidence":"Supply a one-page wireframe plus a short KPI definition for each card."},{"week":9,"level":"Advanced practice","foundation":"An insight distinguishes what happened, what could explain it and what should be checked next. Strong executive writing is concise but evidence-backed.","worked":"“West sales fell 8% in June” is an observation; “stockouts caused it” needs inventory evidence. Recommend checking stock availability, not claiming causation.","extension":"Turn three observations into a 150-word leadership brief: finding, supporting figure, limitation and proposed next action.","evidence":"Every claim must trace to a verified number; label hypotheses and state one limitation."},{"week":10,"level":"Advanced practice","foundation":"Capstone work combines question design, cleaning, calculations, visuals and a decision-ready recommendation. Auditable work matters more than decorative polish.","worked":"Investigate completed-order revenue by region and month, reconcile row counts, compute KPIs and build a small annotated dashboard.","extension":"Peer-review your own report by intentionally changing one filter and checking that source totals, charts and written conclusions update consistently.","evidence":"Submit source, transformation log, KPI definitions, dashboard, executive note and independent verification evidence."}],"excel":[{"week":1,"level":"Start here","foundation":"A workbook contains worksheets, cells, formulas and tables. Learn where the Name Box, Ribbon, formula bar and sheet tabs are before using shortcuts.","worked":"Enter 150 in B2 and 3 in C2, then write =B2*C2 in D2; the calculated total should be 450.","extension":"Create a Raw sheet and an Analysis sheet, and keep inputs separate from formulas. Rename sheets so a colleague can navigate without guessing.","evidence":"Point out the active cell and formula bar, then explain the difference between stored values and computed results."},{"week":2,"level":"Start here","foundation":"Clean analysis begins with consistent columns, correct types and an Excel Table. Display formatting is not the same as underlying values.","worked":"Convert a range containing order_id, region, quantity and unit_price_ngn into a Table using Ctrl+T; format price as ₦ without changing the source number.","extension":"Add a new order and verify that the Table and any structured references expand automatically. Check dates are dates, not imported text.","evidence":"Share a named Table with unmerged headings, stable column names and numeric prices that still calculate."},{"week":3,"level":"Build your skills","foundation":"Formulas use references that change when copied. A relative reference like B2 moves; an absolute reference like $F$1 stays fixed.","worked":"For quantity in B2, price in C2 and tax rate in F1, =B2*C2*(1+$F$1) keeps the tax cell fixed when copied.","extension":"Compare SUM, AVERAGE and COUNT on the same range. Introduce IFERROR only after you identify the error source rather than hiding bad data.","evidence":"Copy the calculation down five rows and explain precisely why the rate reference must remain absolute."},{"week":4,"level":"Build your skills","foundation":"Messy source data needs transparent transformation rules. TRIM removes extra spaces; type conversion makes dates and amounts calculable; UNIQUE is not always equivalent to deleting duplicates.","worked":"Clean \"  North  \" with =TRIM(A2); validate that all regions match an allowed list and flag an unexpected blank.","extension":"Demonstrate the difference between a duplicated order_id and multiple legitimate order lines. Preserve source records and review errors before removal.","evidence":"Show original, cleaned and issue-flag columns with before/after counts."},{"week":5,"level":"Build your skills","foundation":"Conditional analysis combines a rule with the correct denominator. SUMIFS totals selected rows; COUNTIFS counts them; XLOOKUP returns a matching attribute.","worked":"=SUMIFS(Sales[Revenue],Sales[Region],\"West\",Sales[Status],\"Completed\") sums only eligible revenue after adding a calculated Revenue column.","extension":"Add a case-insensitive status normalization and test the lookup with one missing customer; return a clear “Not found” result rather than silently mapping it.","evidence":"Reconcile filtered totals with a PivotTable and explain any difference."},{"week":6,"level":"Apply professionally","foundation":"PivotTables aggregate quickly, while dynamic arrays and LET make calculations reusable. The source grain determines what the PivotTable totals represent.","worked":"Put Region in Rows, Status in Filters and Revenue in Values; check the Values field says Sum, not Count.","extension":"Refresh the pivot after adding records, use slicers, and compare UNIQUE/FILTER results with the corresponding PivotTable.","evidence":"Produce a refreshable summary that agrees with an independent SUMIFS check."},{"week":7,"level":"Apply professionally","foundation":"Use line charts for trends and bar charts for category rankings. Chart titles, axis units and number formatting should serve the question.","worked":"Plot monthly completed revenue as a line and sort regional revenue descending in a horizontal bar chart.","extension":"Remove 3D effects, use a consistent currency label, and compare target-versus-actual with appropriately scaled axes.","evidence":"Verify three plotted points directly against the summary table and write one restrained observation."},{"week":8,"level":"Apply professionally","foundation":"An interactive dashboard connects metrics, visuals and filters to a manager’s decision. Its layout should have clear reading order and no unexplained calculations.","worked":"Place Total Revenue, Gross Profit and Margin above monthly trend and regional ranking; connect the relevant PivotTables to a Region slicer.","extension":"Test every slicer, document what is filtered and protect formula cells from accidental edits. Keep a separate calculation sheet for auditability.","evidence":"Demonstrate a filter change that updates all intended metrics and retains correct denominators."},{"week":9,"level":"Advanced practice","foundation":"A management report translates worksheets into decisions. Use a short headline, verified KPIs, trend context and explicit limitations.","worked":"“Revenue grew 6% versus May” should link to two visible monthly totals, not a manually typed headline with no source.","extension":"Add a check sheet that independently reconciles revenue, order counts and returns. Prepare a print-ready one-page summary.","evidence":"Show an executive page and a validation checklist with no unexplained variance."},{"week":10,"level":"Advanced practice","foundation":"The capstone is a repeatable workbook, not merely a collection of charts. Inputs, cleaning rules, formulas, analysis and recommendations must be traceable.","worked":"Import the practice sales CSV; build a named Table, calculated Revenue and Profit fields, PivotTables and a dashboard.","extension":"Add new rows and demonstrate that calculations, refreshes and charts remain correct without hand-editing visuals.","evidence":"Submit workbook, dashboard snapshot, a definition sheet and evidence of hand-checked totals."}],"sql":[{"week":1,"level":"Start here","foundation":"A relational database stores rows in tables; SQL asks questions of those tables. The schema and data types decide which queries are valid.","worked":"Open the SQL editor, inspect a table using SELECT * FROM orders LIMIT 5, and identify order_id and status as different types of fields.","extension":"Explore table grain and read-only querying. Explain why SELECT is safer for practice than a command that changes shared records.","evidence":"Identify your database dialect, table names, record grain and one safe exploratory query."},{"week":2,"level":"Start here","foundation":"SELECT chooses columns; WHERE filters records; ORDER BY sorts the result; LIMIT restricts output length. Database tables have no guaranteed display order.","worked":"SELECT order_id, quantity*unit_price*(1-discount_pct) AS revenue FROM orders WHERE status = 'Completed' ORDER BY revenue DESC LIMIT 10; uses the separate retail SQL practice schema.","extension":"Add a tie-breaker ORDER BY revenue DESC, order_id ASC so top-ten output is deterministic. Test with a different region and compare row counts.","evidence":"Confirm returned statuses, sort order, amount calculation and tie behavior."},{"week":3,"level":"Build your skills","foundation":"Aggregation changes the level of detail. GROUP BY defines the result grain while WHERE selects rows before aggregation and HAVING filters grouped results.","worked":"SELECT region, SUM(quantity*unit_price*(1-discount_pct)) AS revenue FROM orders WHERE status='Completed' GROUP BY region; gives one row per region.","extension":"Contrast COUNT(*) with COUNT(DISTINCT order_id) on order-line data and explain when they differ.","evidence":"Verify that the sum of regional revenue equals the independently filtered order total."},{"week":4,"level":"Build your skills","foundation":"A JOIN combines datasets using keys, but a many-to-many join can silently multiply revenue. Check uniqueness on the dimension side first.","worked":"Join orders.customer_id to customers.customer_id, then compare order row counts before and after the LEFT JOIN.","extension":"Introduce an unmatched-key diagnostic and distinguish INNER JOIN exclusions from LEFT JOIN null matches.","evidence":"Show pre-/post-join row counts, missing-key count and proof that total revenue did not multiply."},{"week":5,"level":"Build your skills","foundation":"CASE expresses conditional labels; NULL means an unknown or absent value, not numeric zero. SQL three-valued logic affects filters.","worked":"CASE WHEN quantity >= 5 THEN 'Bulk' ELSE 'Standard' END AS order_size groups orders; COALESCE(region,'Unassigned') labels missing regions.","extension":"Test a predicate involving NULL: region = NULL will never match; use IS NULL. Keep business rules explicit and review boundary cases.","evidence":"Explain how all boundary values, missing regions and status rules are classified."},{"week":6,"level":"Apply professionally","foundation":"CTEs make multi-stage analysis readable and testable. Each stage should have one purpose and a stable grain.","worked":"WITH paid AS (SELECT * FROM orders WHERE status='Completed') SELECT region, SUM(quantity*unit_price*(1-discount_pct)) AS revenue FROM paid GROUP BY region; separates the filter from aggregation.","extension":"Create separate CTEs for cleaning and KPI calculation. Compare the final result to a one-query version; beware repeated scans and unsupported dialect syntax.","evidence":"Show intermediate row counts and identical KPI totals in both implementations."},{"week":7,"level":"Apply professionally","foundation":"Window functions retain row-level detail while calculating peer ranks, rolling metrics or period changes. PARTITION BY defines groups; ORDER BY defines sequencing.","worked":"SELECT region, order_id, ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY region ORDER BY quantity*unit_price_ngn DESC, order_id) AS rn FROM orders; ranks records within regions.","extension":"Compare ROW_NUMBER with RANK on tied amounts and use a month-level CTE before applying LAG for month-over-month change.","evidence":"Identify ties, periods with no data, and denominator handling in percentage changes."},{"week":8,"level":"Apply professionally","foundation":"A dashboard-ready dataset has a documented grain and stable metric definitions. Its columns are chosen to support visuals and filters.","worked":"Construct one row per month and region with revenue, order_count and average_order_value from completed records.","extension":"Add a date dimension or generate a month calendar to expose missing months; separate values from formatted strings so BI tools can aggregate correctly.","evidence":"Deliver the dataset schema, query and a check that grand totals match the detailed orders."},{"week":9,"level":"Advanced practice","foundation":"Query performance comes after correctness. Read the execution plan, avoid selecting unused columns and apply predicates on appropriate source fields.","worked":"Compare a full-table SELECT * with a query selecting order_date, region and revenue for a defined reporting period.","extension":"Use EXPLAIN or your editor’s query plan and document scan or join costs without assuming an index always wins. Recheck output after optimization.","evidence":"Present evidence that the optimized query returns the same rows, totals and null handling."},{"week":10,"level":"Advanced practice","foundation":"A reliable SQL capstone includes a readable script, quality checks, reproducible extracts and a management explanation.","worked":"Build an order-to-region monthly report from the practice database with exclusions, join checks and a final summary export.","extension":"Have a reviewer run the script from a fresh database copy. Add comments for grain, filters, keys and validation queries.","evidence":"Submit SQL, data dictionary, output extract, reconciled totals and a one-page insight report."}],"powerbi":[{"week":1,"level":"Start here","foundation":"Power BI Desktop separates Report, Table and Model views. Its strengths depend on trustworthy data and a model, not on dragging random visuals onto a canvas.","worked":"Import the sales CSV, locate the Fields pane and add a simple table showing region and quantity.","extension":"Change a slicer and inspect which visuals react; learn the difference between a visual-level, page-level and report-level filter.","evidence":"Demonstrate where data is loaded, transformed, modelled and rendered."},{"week":2,"level":"Start here","foundation":"Power Query shapes data before it enters the model. Power BI reports do not repair incorrect source types automatically.","worked":"Set order_date to Date, quantity to Whole Number and unit_price_ngn to Decimal Number; inspect invalid rows.","extension":"Create a step-by-step query that normalizes status labels without deleting the original source. Refresh it twice to verify repeatability.","evidence":"Show applied steps, row-count checks, and a successful refresh with expected types."},{"week":3,"level":"Build your skills","foundation":"Star schemas put measurable transactions in fact tables and descriptive attributes in dimension tables. Relationship direction changes the meaning of filters.","worked":"Create FactSales keyed by customer_id and a unique DimCustomer; test that selecting a customer segment filters sales without multiplying rows.","extension":"Detect duplicate dimension keys, avoid ambiguous bidirectional relationships and understand why a date dimension improves time reporting.","evidence":"Draw the relationship model and reconcile fact revenue before and after filtering."},{"week":4,"level":"Build your skills","foundation":"A DAX measure is evaluated inside filter context; a calculated column is stored row by row. CALCULATE changes filter context deliberately.","worked":"Revenue = SUMX(FactSales, FactSales[quantity] * FactSales[unit_price_ngn]) computes a measure at the appropriate grain.","extension":"Use CALCULATE([Revenue], FactSales[status]=\"Completed\") and check whether slicers should remain active. Compare with an equivalent filtered source total.","evidence":"Explain why the same measure returns different values by region without being rewritten."},{"week":5,"level":"Build your skills","foundation":"Time intelligence requires a complete, correctly related Date table. Comparisons must align periods and distinguish partial months.","worked":"Create a marked Date table and use DATEADD('Date'[Date],-1,MONTH) inside CALCULATE for a prior-month measure.","extension":"Compare month-over-month absolute change and rate; guard division by zero and flag an incomplete current period.","evidence":"Verify a selected month against two visible source totals and document date-range assumptions."},{"week":6,"level":"Apply professionally","foundation":"Advanced DAX balances readability, context control and performance. Variables simplify repeated expressions, while ALL and REMOVEFILTERS alter denominators.","worked":"Margin % = DIVIDE([Gross Profit],[Revenue]) changes correctly with region filters; use VAR to hold intermediate results in an extended calculation.","extension":"Build a contribution-to-total measure and explain why the denominator deliberately ignores a region filter. Avoid overusing ALL on dimensions that users expect to filter.","evidence":"Provide a context test matrix for region, month and blank selections."},{"week":7,"level":"Apply professionally","foundation":"Each Power BI visual should have an analytical purpose and accessible interaction. A dashboard with dozens of charts is usually less useful than a few precise comparisons.","worked":"Use a KPI card for Revenue, a line chart for monthly trend and a sorted bar chart for regional contribution.","extension":"Test cross-filtering, tooltip definitions and accessible labels. Flag that a bookmark is a view state, not a new dataset.","evidence":"Link every visual to a question and independently verify at least two plotted values."},{"week":8,"level":"Apply professionally","foundation":"Report design combines a clear story with stable measures. Consistent navigation, filter feedback and responsive readability make a dashboard usable.","worked":"Build an executive page with Revenue, Margin %, target variance, trends and a compact detail table.","extension":"Use synchronized date slicers, explicit empty states and mobile layout where supported. Test the report with one region that has sparse data.","evidence":"Deliver a functioning page with no misleading blank KPI and documented calculations."},{"week":9,"level":"Advanced practice","foundation":"Publishing introduces refresh, sharing and data-governance responsibilities. A desktop report is not the same as a governed shared semantic model.","worked":"Explain the difference between a local PBIX, a published semantic model and a report in the Power BI service.","extension":"Set a refresh schedule only after confirming credentials, gateways and data classification. Discuss workspace permissions and row-level security as separate controls.","evidence":"Produce a deployment checklist with dataset owner, refresh monitoring and data access decisions."},{"week":10,"level":"Advanced practice","foundation":"The capstone connects source profiling, a star schema, DAX measures, dashboard design and communication.","worked":"Deliver a sales report with a Date table, documented revenue and margin measures, interactive filters and an executive summary.","extension":"Validate published figures against a source extract, test role-based access where configured and document a refresh plan.","evidence":"Submit report source, model diagram, KPI definitions, validation evidence and stakeholder recommendations."}],"tableau":[{"week":1,"level":"Start here","foundation":"Tableau distinguishes the Data Source, Worksheet, Dashboard and Story workspaces. You create views by placing fields on shelves, not by writing SQL for every chart.","worked":"Connect a CSV, drag Region to Rows and SUM(quantity) to Columns, and observe the generated horizontal bar chart; revenue is calculated in a later week.","extension":"Replace SUM with AVG and explain why the answer changes. Check field roles and granularity before formatting marks.","evidence":"Identify the shelf, Marks card, data pane and current aggregation, then verify a displayed value from the source."},{"week":2,"level":"Start here","foundation":"Dimensions split data into categories, measures produce aggregations, and relationships or joins affect record counts.","worked":"Use Region as a dimension and quantity as a measure. Tableau can show SUM(quantity) or AVG(quantity), two valid but different metrics.","extension":"Build an OrderID distinct count and compare with row count. Test whether joining an order-level table to order lines duplicates amounts.","evidence":"Write a one-sentence field definition and aggregation rule for each output measure."},{"week":3,"level":"Build your skills","foundation":"Visual design begins with a comparison question. Tableau makes charts quickly, but the analyst chooses meaningful axes, units and order.","worked":"Build a sorted regional sales bar chart, then a monthly revenue line. Highlight only an important comparison rather than applying many unrelated colors.","extension":"Compare how different date grains (day vs month) change the trend shape. Use tooltips that display units and source context.","evidence":"Verify one bar, one trend point and the time grain against the underlying data."},{"week":4,"level":"Build your skills","foundation":"Calculated fields express reusable business logic. Aggregating before or after calculating changes results for many ratios.","worked":"Create [Line Revenue] = [quantity] * [unit_price_ngn] and then SUM([Line Revenue]) on the view.","extension":"For margin, divide SUM([Profit]) by SUM([Revenue]) rather than average per-row margin percentages without a reason. Test zero denominators and null source fields.","evidence":"Document each calculated field and compare it with an independent workbook total."},{"week":5,"level":"Build your skills","foundation":"Filters and parameters serve different purposes. Filters restrict records or marks; parameters hold a user-selected value used in calculations or logic.","worked":"Add a Region filter, then a parameter to switch between SUM(Revenue) and SUM(Quantity) using a CASE calculation.","extension":"Describe how a context filter can affect subsequent filters and why dashboard actions sometimes change an unrelated view.","evidence":"Test default, empty and multi-select states and report the behavior clearly."},{"week":6,"level":"Apply professionally","foundation":"A level-of-detail expression can calculate at a grain different from the visual. FIXED, INCLUDE and EXCLUDE answer different questions.","worked":"{FIXED [Customer ID] : SUM([Line Revenue])} computes a customer total regardless of the view’s dimensions, subject to Tableau’s order of operations.","extension":"Compare a customer-level FIXED calculation shown by region with a plain SUM; test a context filter and explain why the totals differ.","evidence":"Name the calculation grain and list which filters affect it."},{"week":7,"level":"Apply professionally","foundation":"Table calculations operate over marks in the displayed view, so sort order and addressing matter.","worked":"Use RUNNING_SUM(SUM([Line Revenue])) on a monthly trend, then inspect how restarting the calculation by Region changes the result.","extension":"Compare a table-calculation percent-of-total with a source-level share and identify when removed marks distort interpretation.","evidence":"Show the addressing direction, partitioning and a hand-checked running total."},{"week":8,"level":"Apply professionally","foundation":"A Tableau dashboard is a responsive composition of worksheets, filters and actions. Containers help align objects without relying on arbitrary pixel positions.","worked":"Arrange revenue KPI, monthly trend and region ranking inside vertical and horizontal containers, with one clearly labeled filter panel.","extension":"Add a click action and test on a narrow screen; make legends and empty states comprehensible.","evidence":"Provide a dashboard screenshot and an interaction test plan linking each control to intended changes."},{"week":9,"level":"Advanced practice","foundation":"Story points guide an audience through evidence. A visual change should answer a question, not just animate a chart.","worked":"Create three Tableau Story points: overall revenue trend, the key regional difference, and the recommended follow-up.","extension":"State why a pattern is not proof of cause, and annotate exclusions and sample coverage. Present a short executive narrative with figures verified independently.","evidence":"Deliver an evidence-linked story that separates findings, limitations and recommendations."},{"week":10,"level":"Advanced practice","foundation":"The capstone demonstrates a complete Tableau analytics workflow from connection to a reproducible dashboard and business story.","worked":"Import the practice data, validate dimensions and measures, create calculated KPIs, and publish or export an interactive workbook where permissible.","extension":"Recheck output after changing a filter and record view-grain assumptions and privacy decisions before sharing.","evidence":"Submit the workbook, calculation glossary, dashboard/story evidence and two independent total checks."}],"powerquery":[{"week":1,"level":"Start here","foundation":"Power Query is a repeatable transformation engine in Excel and Power BI. The Query Editor previews data and records each action as an Applied Step.","worked":"Open Get Data, import the sample sales CSV and locate the Query Settings, Applied Steps, column type icon and preview grid.","extension":"Duplicate the original query rather than manually deleting source columns, and identify the refresh entry point.","evidence":"Demonstrate the difference between the source file, a transformation step and the loaded output table."},{"week":2,"level":"Start here","foundation":"Correct data types affect joins, comparisons and arithmetic. Date parsing can vary by locale, while IDs should not become integers.","worked":"Type order_date as Date, unit_price_ngn as Decimal Number and customer_id as Text, then inspect the error indicator on every converted column.","extension":"Re-import a date that is ambiguous between day/month and month/day using explicit locale. Explain why 0012 should stay text when it is an ID.","evidence":"Provide a type map, observed error counts and one corrected locale example."},{"week":3,"level":"Build your skills","foundation":"Null values, duplicate rows and errors are different problems. Replacing every null with zero can create false business data.","worked":"Flag missing region as “Unassigned” only when that is a defensible reporting rule; preserve an issue counter and the original source.","extension":"Compare removing duplicate full rows against distinct order_id values; multiple items in one order might be legitimate.","evidence":"Show original counts, issue flags, transformed counts and a reason for each treatment."},{"week":4,"level":"Build your skills","foundation":"Split, trim, clean and date functions standardize messy text or timestamps. Transformations should handle new records predictably at refresh.","worked":"Apply Text.Trim to a region column containing “ West ”, then use Date.Month([order_date]) to produce a month number after conversion.","extension":"Test inconsistent capitalization, invisible spaces and invalid dates; decide whether errors should be fixed, excluded or surfaced for review.","evidence":"Provide before/after samples and a refresh test with one deliberately messy new row."},{"week":5,"level":"Build your skills","foundation":"Conditional columns create classifications from explicit business rules. Custom columns use M expressions and reference fields by name.","worked":"Add a custom [Line Revenue] = [quantity] * [unit_price_ngn] and a conditional [Order Type] classifying quantity >= 5 as Bulk.","extension":"Test the exact boundary at 4 and 5, then inspect null quantities and negative prices. Avoid replacing missing values with plausible-looking outputs.","evidence":"Document the rule, exceptions and a verified sample of resulting columns."},{"week":6,"level":"Apply professionally","foundation":"Append stacks compatible records; combining monthly files requires robust schema and source tracking. A folder refresh should not silently drop renamed columns.","worked":"Append January and February order files with matching column types and a SourceMonth field.","extension":"Test a third file missing a field and create a diagnostic query listing schema deviations. Maintain a rejected-file log instead of concealing errors.","evidence":"Reconcile input and output row counts by file and show a clean refresh after adding a month."},{"week":7,"level":"Apply professionally","foundation":"Merge enriches fact records with lookup attributes. Join kind and uniqueness determine what rows survive or multiply.","worked":"Merge orders to the unique customer lookup on customer_id with a Left Outer join; expand customer segment after checking key uniqueness.","extension":"Use an anti-join diagnostic to list orders without matches. Compare before/after record counts and revenue totals.","evidence":"Deliver matched, unmatched and duplicate-key counts with a clear merge rule."},{"week":8,"level":"Apply professionally","foundation":"Group By summarizes data; pivot and unpivot reshape data into a useful analytical grain.","worked":"Group sales by Region and Month, computing Sum of Revenue and Count of orders, then unpivot a wide KPI table into Metric and Value columns.","extension":"Verify whether the source has one row per order or per order line before using Count Rows. Compare one grouped figure to a hand-calculated sample.","evidence":"Explain output grain and submit row counts and reconciliation totals."},{"week":9,"level":"Advanced practice","foundation":"M is the language behind Applied Steps. Good queries are named, parameterized and refreshable; failures should be observable.","worked":"Read a simple let ... in query from top to bottom, and identify a Source step, Changed Type step and a custom transformation.","extension":"Introduce a folder path parameter and a safe error-check step; document credentials and refresh dependencies without exposing secrets.","evidence":"Show that a fresh import produces the same final schema and totals."},{"week":10,"level":"Advanced practice","foundation":"The final pipeline must survive updated data, explain transformations and deliver reliable model inputs.","worked":"Prepare monthly sales files, normalize types, flag invalid records, append periods and merge a product lookup.","extension":"Test schema drift by adding a file with a missing column and verify the error diagnostics.","evidence":"Submit Power Query project, transformation notes, clean export, rejection summary and reconciliation checks."}],"statistics":[{"week":1,"level":"Start here","foundation":"Statistics helps answer questions about data while distinguishing observations from the wider population. Start by separating categories, numeric measures and the unit being counted.","worked":"In a sample of five order values, 4, 6, 6, 8 and 26 (thousands), identify currency as quantitative and region as categorical.","extension":"Decide whether the data contains a census of all orders or a sample, and list how sampling affects conclusions.","evidence":"State the population, observation unit and variable types before choosing a statistic."},{"week":2,"level":"Start here","foundation":"The mean uses all values, the median is the middle ordered value and the mode is most frequent. They answer different versions of “typical.”","worked":"For 4, 6, 6, 8 and 26, mean = 10, median = 6 and mode = 6; the outlier pulls the mean upward.","extension":"Group values into regions and compare their means with their medians; discuss whether a weighted mean is appropriate.","evidence":"Compute each statistic correctly and explain which best describes a skewed distribution."},{"week":3,"level":"Build your skills","foundation":"Spread is as important as centre. Range captures extremes; variance and standard deviation describe dispersion around the mean.","worked":"Values 8, 10 and 12 have mean 10; their sample standard deviation is 2, whereas the population standard deviation is approximately 1.63.","extension":"Contrast Excel STDEV.S and STDEV.P and explain the n-1 denominator for sample variance.","evidence":"Report the metric, sample size, units and why the chosen measure of spread fits the question."},{"week":4,"level":"Build your skills","foundation":"Frequency tables reveal distribution shape, typical ranges and surprising tails. Histogram bins are intervals, not categories such as product type.","worked":"Group order values into equal-width bands and count occurrences; total bar counts must equal the nonmissing sample size.","extension":"Draw a box plot and investigate extreme values without automatically declaring them mistakes. Check whether different bin widths create misleading impressions.","evidence":"Submit a frequency table, a labelled plot and a short interpretation of skewness or outliers."},{"week":5,"level":"Build your skills","foundation":"A sample can be precise yet biased. Random sampling, stratification, selection and non-response determine what findings can generalize to.","worked":"Surveying only weekday shoppers misses weekend behavior even with 500 responses.","extension":"Design a stratified sample by region, document strata weights and compare it with a convenience sample. Explain that a larger biased sample does not remove selection bias.","evidence":"Identify sampling frame, inclusion criteria, likely bias and an appropriate mitigation."},{"week":6,"level":"Apply professionally","foundation":"Probability expresses uncertainty, not certainty of a specific outcome. Conditional probability depends on the subgroup used as the denominator.","worked":"If 20 of 100 orders are late, P(Late)=0.20. If 8 of 20 express orders are late, P(Late given Express)=0.40.","extension":"Build a two-way frequency table and distinguish P(A|B) from P(B|A). Check for mutually exclusive and independent events before multiplying probabilities.","evidence":"Show numerator, denominator, event definition and the resulting probability."},{"week":7,"level":"Apply professionally","foundation":"Correlation measures association, usually between two numeric variables. It does not establish cause, direction of influence or freedom from confounding.","worked":"A correlation of r=0.80 between advertising spend and sales is a positive linear association; a seasonal factor may influence both.","extension":"Draw a scatter plot, check outliers and consider a lagged relationship or a third variable. Compare Pearson with Spearman where ranks are more suitable.","evidence":"State r, sample size, plot pattern, limitation and one plausible alternative explanation."},{"week":8,"level":"Apply professionally","foundation":"A confidence interval describes a procedure that captures the population parameter in a specified fraction of repeated samples. It is not a probability assigned to a fixed unknown parameter.","worked":"If a sample mean is 100, standard error 5, and a normal approximation is reasonable, a 95% interval is about 100 ± 1.96×5 = [90.2,109.8].","extension":"Explore how doubling sample size generally narrows standard error; discuss skewed samples and when a t interval is needed.","evidence":"Report estimate, interval, method, assumptions and the population measured."},{"week":9,"level":"Advanced practice","foundation":"Hypothesis tests evaluate whether observed data are inconsistent with a clearly stated null under model assumptions. A p-value is not the probability the null is true.","worked":"Compare two conversion rates: 40/200=20% and 52/200=26%. The observed difference is six percentage points; significance still needs an appropriate test and uncertainty.","extension":"Describe random assignment, practical significance, multiple tests and sample-size effects before making a business recommendation.","evidence":"Provide null/alternative hypotheses, chosen test, uncertainty and a cautious conclusion."},{"week":10,"level":"Advanced practice","foundation":"The statistics capstone connects exploratory analysis, an explicit research question, uncertainty and a reproducible executive explanation.","worked":"Analyze regional order values, show centre/spread and distribution, then evaluate one defensible comparison using the appropriate interval or test.","extension":"Test assumptions, report missingness and sensitivity to outliers, and avoid causal claims without an experimental design.","evidence":"Submit calculations, plots, assumptions, validation and a recommendation that reflects uncertainty."}],"python":[{"week":1,"level":"Start here","foundation":"Python executes statements in sequence; notebooks and editors show inputs, outputs and errors. A variable holds a value and a function performs a reusable operation.","worked":"Run revenue = 3 * 2500 followed by print(revenue); the result is 7500, and the variable name makes the calculation readable.","extension":"Intentionally trigger a NameError, inspect the traceback, then correct the variable name. Save a .py script rather than relying on unsaved notebook state.","evidence":"Explain variable, expression, print output and error traceback using one short working program."},{"week":2,"level":"Start here","foundation":"Python types control what operations are valid. Lists hold ordered elements; dictionaries map keys to values; control flow applies conditions.","worked":"prices = [1200, 1800, 2500]; sum(prices) is 5500. A loop can identify prices above 1500, while a function can encapsulate the rule.","extension":"Write a reusable function that returns discounted price for a valid numeric input; reject negative quantities and test boundary values.","evidence":"Show three inputs, expected outputs and evidence that the function handles invalid data intentionally."},{"week":3,"level":"Build your skills","foundation":"Pandas reads tabular data into DataFrames with columns and rows. Inspect fields and types before manipulating the records.","worked":"import pandas as pd; df = pd.read_csv('learning_sales_2026.csv'); print(df[['order_id','region']].head()); uses the provided dataset.","extension":"Convert order_date with pd.to_datetime(errors='coerce'), validate key uniqueness expectations and compare df.shape before and after filtering.","evidence":"Report the original row count, dtypes, null counts and a five-row sample."},{"week":4,"level":"Build your skills","foundation":"Data cleaning should make failures visible instead of hiding them. Pandas null handling, string normalization and conversions require specific policies.","worked":"df['region'] = df['region'].astype('string').str.strip(); creates a normalized region field; flag blanks before filling.","extension":"Compare drop_duplicates() with drop_duplicates(subset=['order_id']) only after documenting grain. Avoid chained assignment and preserve the original file for reproducibility.","evidence":"Display issue counts and explain exactly which records changed or were excluded."},{"week":5,"level":"Build your skills","foundation":"GroupBy performs split-apply-combine analysis, and derived columns define the measures being summarized.","worked":"df['revenue'] = df['quantity'] * df['unit_price_ngn']; completed = df[df['status'].str.strip().str.lower() == 'completed']; completed.groupby('region')['revenue'].sum() totals eligible sales.","extension":"Check missing regions and compare a grouped total with completed['revenue'].sum(); never average ratios without considering weights.","evidence":"Provide a table by region, the business definition of revenue and an independent reconciliation."},{"week":6,"level":"Apply professionally","foundation":"Merging DataFrames uses join keys and can accidentally multiply records. Test cardinality before choosing a merge.","worked":"df.merge(customers, on='customer_id', how='left', validate='many_to_one', indicator=True) checks an expected many-to-one relationship.","extension":"Count left_only records and investigate duplicate keys on the customer side. Compare before/after row counts and revenue so silent multiplication is caught.","evidence":"Deliver a merge audit showing matched, unmatched and invalid relationships."},{"week":7,"level":"Apply professionally","foundation":"Charts are analytic evidence, not decoration. Use correct chart types, labels and scales; always explain the measured population.","worked":"Compute monthly completed revenue with a grouped DataFrame and plot months in chronological order; use a horizontal bar for region comparison.","extension":"Introduce error bars when the data represent estimates and note that visual trends cannot by themselves establish causality.","evidence":"Validate two plotted values against the grouped table and annotate units and exclusions."},{"week":8,"level":"Apply professionally","foundation":"A reproducible Python report should expose inputs, transformations, results and assumptions. Notebook code order must be runnable from a fresh kernel.","worked":"Build a notebook with Load, Quality Check, KPI Calculation, Visualization and Interpretation sections; export an HTML/PDF report where possible.","extension":"Re-run all cells from the start and verify identical outputs. Keep charts within readable widths and separate figures from observations and recommendations.","evidence":"Share a reproducibility checklist and evidence that a clean run succeeds."},{"week":9,"level":"Advanced practice","foundation":"Reliable analysis code includes readable functions, focused tests and explicit dependency versions. Performance comes after correct results.","worked":"Create load_sales(), validate_sales() and summarize_sales() functions, then assert that summed regional sales equals filtered total within a numeric tolerance.","extension":"Handle empty files and nonnumeric price values without silently ignoring bad data; consider vectorized pandas operations instead of row loops.","evidence":"Present tests for normal, missing and boundary cases, plus documented environment requirements."},{"week":10,"level":"Advanced practice","foundation":"The capstone connects data preparation, metrics, checks and communication in one repeatable Python package.","worked":"Read the fictional sales CSV, normalize statuses, calculate revenue and profit, produce a monthly report, and save charts and a summary.","extension":"Include a README, run instructions, validation assertions and a sensitivity check for missing values.","evidence":"Submit code/notebook, input references, reproducible outputs and a concise evidence-based executive recommendation."}],"ai":[{"week":1,"level":"Start here","foundation":"AI assists analysis but is not a source of truth. Begin with a clear task, trusted inputs, allowed operations and a human verification plan.","worked":"A prompt should state: “Using the fictional monthly sales table, propose three relevant KPIs; do not invent any values; identify missing fields.”","extension":"Compare a vague prompt with a specific one and mark any answer that asserts facts not in the supplied data.","evidence":"Deliver a task-specific prompt and a checklist separating generated suggestions from verified evidence."},{"week":2,"level":"Start here","foundation":"A good prompt supplies context, role, objective, source schema, constraints and output format. Prompt iterations should change one variable at a time.","worked":"“Return a table with metric, formula, required columns and verification check; use quantity and unit_price_ngn only” constrains the assistant to known fields.","extension":"Ask for assumptions before solutions and require uncertainty to be labelled. Test what happens when a needed field is absent.","evidence":"Provide versioned prompts and an output review that catches invented columns."},{"week":3,"level":"Build your skills","foundation":"AI can draft a data-cleaning plan, but it cannot infer what a missing business value truly means. Human decisions must govern exclusions and replacements.","worked":"Ask the assistant to classify blank region, malformed dates and mixed-case status labels, then review each proposed treatment against a small sample.","extension":"Compare a generic “remove duplicates” instruction with a grain-aware rule that preserves legitimate multi-line orders. Record how many rows each action affects.","evidence":"Submit a cleaning plan with specific rules, reversibility and a rejection log."},{"week":4,"level":"Build your skills","foundation":"AI can explain formulas and help draft spreadsheet logic if you supply exact column names and expected results. Calculations still require a real engine.","worked":"Request =SUMIFS(Sales[Revenue],Sales[Region],\"West\",Sales[Status],\"Completed\") and compare its result with a manually filtered sample; Revenue must be a defined calculated column.","extension":"Try one intentionally wrong formula and ask the assistant to explain why it fails. Verify that replacing a missing field with a guess is unacceptable.","evidence":"Deliver the working formula, test data, expected value and a check independent of the AI."},{"week":5,"level":"Build your skills","foundation":"AI can help write readable SQL, but generated SQL may reference nonexistent fields or change the query grain. Never run unreviewed write queries.","worked":"Provide the exact source schema and request a SELECT-only query for completed revenue by region; validate it in the SQL Practice Arena.","extension":"Check that joins preserve expected rows, filters are correct, and sums match an independent calculation. Reject DROP, UPDATE or fabricated tables in a learning query.","evidence":"Show final SQL, row count, total reconciliation and a summary of corrections made to AI output."},{"week":6,"level":"Apply professionally","foundation":"AI can explain a Python traceback and propose repairs, but executable code must be inspected and tested.","worked":"Supply a short pandas groupby error with fictional data, ask for a minimal repair, then run the corrected code in the Python editor.","extension":"Request a test that catches the same issue in future and confirm any libraries exist in the environment. Keep external secrets and personal data out of prompts.","evidence":"Record the original error, corrected line, passing test and why the repair works."},{"week":7,"level":"Apply professionally","foundation":"AI is useful for proposing EDA questions and visual narratives, not for making unsupported causal claims.","worked":"Give it verified monthly sales totals and ask for the most appropriate trend and category charts, then inspect the generated explanation for arithmetic errors.","extension":"Challenge the model with a deliberately misleading chart scale and ask it to identify the flaw. Compare suggestions with data granularity and audience needs.","evidence":"Deliver two evidence-backed chart choices and an annotated limitation for each."},{"week":8,"level":"Apply professionally","foundation":"AI-assisted dashboards should begin with decision requirements, verified KPI definitions and a clear wireframe. A layout suggestion is not a deployed dashboard.","worked":"Provide Revenue, Margin % and monthly trend requirements; ask AI for a layout that names each visual’s question, filter and data source.","extension":"Build or sketch the output in an actual BI tool and validate interactions. Do not paste generated numbers as if they came from the dataset.","evidence":"Submit a wireframe, KPI glossary, interaction checklist and explicit human verification record."},{"week":9,"level":"Advanced practice","foundation":"Quality assurance and governance are part of professional AI use. Source provenance, confidentiality and reproducibility determine whether generated content is suitable for an audience.","worked":"Give an AI-written executive summary two conflicting KPI figures and test whether the review catches the mismatch.","extension":"Add a disclosure of AI assistance, a claim-to-evidence register and a human approval step. Discuss prompt injection and never obey instructions embedded in untrusted source text.","evidence":"Submit a corrected report showing evidence for each headline statement and who approved it."},{"week":10,"level":"Advanced practice","foundation":"The AI capstone combines workflow design, technical validation and human accountability. The output must stand even if a reviewer ignores the AI-generated prose.","worked":"Use fictional sales records to draft a cleaning plan, SQL/Python or Excel calculations, chart choices and executive findings; independently run and reconcile every number.","extension":"Save prompt versions, source files, scripts, rejected claims and a quality-control checklist. Identify what AI assisted and what a person verified.","evidence":"Submit a reproducible evidence pack, disclosure, dashboard/story and a decision-ready recommendation."}]};
